T2.11 -- window reductions RE-MEASURED against the shipped entry points
=======================================================================

WHAT THIS FILE IS, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT REPLACE

`window_benchmark.log` in this directory is X-11 / E-3: the experiment that CHOSE
the shapes, measured against copies of them living inside the benchmark. This file
is the re-measurement TASKS.md T2.11 requires after those shapes landed in
ops/reduce.hpp, and it is a separate file on purpose. The pre-split numbers are
not superseded by these -- they answer "what is the accumulator worth", these
answer "what does it buy in the shipped library" -- and T2.11's done-when says in
as many words that replacing one with the other silently is not acceptable.

WHAT CHANGED IN THE BENCHMARK

  * INC-ROW is now bincv::SlidingWindowCount (was a copy in this file)
  * fused covariance is now bincv::countCovariance (was a copy)
  * four-argument split is now bincv::countAndSplit(a,b,c0,c1,r) (was a copy)
  * INC-COL is still a copy -- axis 1 declined to expose it, so there is nothing
    shipped to time
  * NEW: recompute-1acc, the recompute path with T2.11 item 4 UNDONE (one size_t
    across the whole region). Timed interleaved with the other three so that item
    4's own effect is measured the same way everything else here is, instead of by
    comparing absolute ns across two sessions.

WHAT CHANGED IN THE LIBRARY, AND WHY THE RATIOS MOVE

T2.11 item 4 landed FIRST: impl::countViewRegion and the countAnd/countAndSplit
row bodies now each return a per-row partial sum instead of accumulating into one
size_t across the region. That makes the DENOMINATOR of axis 1 and one side of
axis 2 faster, so smaller ratios here are the expected consequence of the change
landing, not a regression. X-11 predicted axis 1 would fall from 7.3x/20x to
"roughly 5.6x/15x". It measured 5.96x and 15.9x.

PLATFORM: the reference device -- AUTHORITATIVE. Every variant here is
popcount-bound except INC-COL, which issues none, so the X-7 lowering is the whole
ratio and x86 cannot rank these at all.

  device:           pi4
  cpu:              Raspberry Pi 4 Model B Rev 1.5 (Cortex-A72)
  arch / kernel:    aarch64 / 6.18.34+rpt-rpi-v8
  compiler:         g++ (Debian 14.2.0-19) 14.2.0, Release (-O2 -DNDEBUG)
  governor:         performance (restored to ondemand on exit)
  core pinning:     taskset -c 3
  throttled before / after: throttled=0x0 / throttled=0x0   (every run)
  repository commit at measurement: 7ae22ba + the uncommitted T2.11 working tree

THREE RUNS. Batch spread within each run was 0.2-3.0% on every row.

