READING AXIS 2. Still past the 15% line at every point, and the decision stands.
uint32_t at 31x31 moved from 1.27x to 1.20x, which is item 4 working as designed
on the side it helps most: the composition makes TWO countNonZero calls and the
fused pass makes none, so splitting the accumulator improved the denominator more
than the numerator. The popcount count is still identical on both sides.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
AXIS 3 -- selector plane versus the four-argument countAndSplit
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ns/keypoint, uint32_t, 200 keypoints (values from run 3):

  W     plane (shipped)   four-arg XOR   run 1   run 2   run 3    X-11
   7             122.5          149.5    0.81x   0.73x   0.82x   0.74x
  15             264.6          312.2    0.83x   0.79x   0.85x   0.77x
  31             591.2          695.4    0.85x   0.83x   0.85x   0.80x

  SPEED, per frame at W=31 and 200 keypoints, formation included:
    plane     591.2 ns x 200 = 118.2 us  +  7.5 us to form the plane  = 125.7 us
    four-arg  695.4 ns x 200 = 139.1 us  +  0                         = 139.1 us
    -> the plane is 11-14% faster per frame per pyramid level across the three
       runs (X-11: 16-18%), even after paying to build it.

  MEMORY: unchanged, and it is what decides this axis.
    selector plane   38400 B at 640x480, scaling with the level -- 9600 B at L1,
                     2400 B at L2, ~51 kB over a 4-level pyramid; a FIFTH plane
                     against the four the covariance already reads, so +25% on the
                     derivative working set of EVERY level, held for the frame.
    four-argument         0 B, at every level

READING AXIS 3. The gap narrowed (11-14% rather than 16-18%) and the direction did
not move, so CLAUDE.md's tiebreak reaches the same place it reached in X-11: the
four-argument form is what T3.6 calls, and the three-argument overload stays for a
caller that already holds a plane and is faster for holding it.

X-7 CAVEAT. Unchanged, and at its strongest here: every variant except INC-COL is
popcount-bound and binCV adds no -march flag, so these ratios are a function of
what a popcount costs relative to a load on this core. x86 cannot rank them.

